Triangle $ABC$ lies in the square base and is right-angled at $B$.

$\begin{aligned}AC^2&=5^2+5^2\\AC^2&=50\\AC&=5\sqrt{2}\text{ m}\end{aligned}$

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (B) at (3,0);
\coordinate (C) at (3,3);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--cycle;
\draw (2.75,0)--(2.75,0.25)--(3,0.25);
\path (A) node[below left] {$A$} (B) node[below right] {$B$} (C) node[above right] {$C$};
\path (1.5,0) node[below] {$5\text{ m}$} (3,1.5) node[right] {$5\text{ m}$} (1.5,1.5) node[above left] {$5\sqrt{2}\text{ m}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

First find the diagonal of the square base.

$\begin{aligned}AC^2&=6^2+6^2\\AC^2&=72\\AC&=6\sqrt{2}\text{ cm}\end{aligned}$

The vertical edge $CD$ is perpendicular to the base. Triangle $ACD$ is right-angled at $C$.

$\begin{aligned}AD^2&=(6\sqrt{2})^2+6^2\\AD^2&=108\\AD&=6\sqrt{3}\text{ cm}\end{aligned}$

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0);
\coordinate (C) at (4,0);
\coordinate (D) at (4,2.8);
\draw (A)--(C)--(D)--cycle;
\draw (3.75,0)--(3.75,0.25)--(4,0.25);
\path (A) node[below left] {$A$} (C) node[below right] {$C$} (D) node[above right] {$D$};
\path (2,0) node[below] {$6\sqrt{2}\text{ cm}$} (4,1.4) node[right] {$6\text{ cm}$} (2,1.4) node[above left] {$6\sqrt{3}\text{ cm}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$RP$ lies in the rectangular face $PSRQ$ and passes through $R$. Since $RU$ is perpendicular to that face, $RU$ is perpendicular to $RP$.

$\angle PRU=90^\circ$.

The relevant triangle is shown separately.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (P) at (0,0);
\coordinate (R) at (3.5,0);
\coordinate (U) at (3.5,2);
\draw (P)--(R)--(U)--cycle;
\draw (3.25,0)--(3.25,0.25)--(3.5,0.25);
\path (P) node[below left] {$P$} (R) node[below right] {$R$} (U) node[above right] {$U$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

(a) $ON$ lies in the base through the foot $O$ of the perpendicular height $VO$, so $\angle VON=90^\circ$.

(b) $OJ$ also lies in the base through $O$, so $\angle VOJ=90^\circ$.

The two vertical slices are shown separately.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (V) at (0,2.5);
\coordinate (N) at (2,0);
\draw (O)--(V)--(N)--cycle;
\draw (0,0.25)--(0.25,0.25)--(0.25,0);
\path (O) node[below left] {$O$} (V) node[above] {$V$} (N) node[below right] {$N$};
\coordinate (O2) at (3.5,0);
\coordinate (V2) at (3.5,2.5);
\coordinate (J) at (6.3,0);
\draw (O2)--(V2)--(J)--cycle;
\draw (3.5,0.25)--(3.75,0.25)--(3.75,0);
\path (O2) node[below left] {$O$} (V2) node[above] {$V$} (J) node[below right] {$J$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
